  "articleBody": "Navigating the world of AI search visibility can feel a bit like uncharted territory, and it's easy to stumble into common pitfalls that hinder your efforts. You want your content to be seen and cited, but certain approaches can actually work against you.\n\nOne big mistake is producing large volumes of lightly edited AI text without proper review. While AI tools can generate content quickly, they can also produce plausible but inaccurate statements or content that lacks your unique business expertise. Google emphasizes original, high-quality content that demonstrates expertise, experience, authoritativeness, and trustworthiness – not just bulk output. Relying solely on AI without human oversight means you're missing that critical human touch.\n\nAnother misstep is copying competitors or simply answering keywords without truly addressing the underlying customer need. AI systems are sophisticated; they're looking for content that genuinely helps users and provides fact-dense answers. If your content merely rehashes what others are saying or focuses too much on keyword stuffing rather than utility, it won't stand out.\n\nForgetting to include first-hand business knowledge or making unsupported claims is also detrimental. Your unique insights and verifiable facts are what differentiate your business. Without them, your content loses its authoritative edge. AI content should build on your real expertise, not replace it.\n\nFinally, treating special AI markup (like schema) as a substitute for useful content is a huge error. Schema is vital for AI to understand your content, but it's not a magic bullet. It enhances good content; it doesn't transform bad content into good. The foundational rule is always 'people-first' content – helpful and reliable information that users actually need.\n\n**When Professional Guidance Helps**\nConsider professional help when your subject matter is technically complex, your internal team lacks writers or reviewers, or factual errors could significantly impact customers. If you need to produce content across many services or locations, or if you're struggling to verify sources and integrate subject-matter expert input, that's a clear signal. A professional can ensure accuracy, maintain quality, and implement a robust review process that AI tools alone can't provide.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
